# 03 Callouts: (?Cn) with pcre2_set_callout()
# https://www.pcre.org/current/doc/html/pcre2callout.html
#
# Not exposed by PHP. The (?C) syntax compiles in preg_* patterns but
# there is no way to register a handler, so callouts do nothing there.
#
# What it is: (?Cn) marks points in a pattern. At match time the engine
# calls user code at every mark it passes and hands over the callout
# number, the current subject position and the match state so far. The
# callback then decides. It can let matching continue or fail just this
# path to force backtracking. It can also abort the whole match.
#
# What it enables: tracing a match as the engine walks it, or aborting
# one that runs too long. Also predicates in the middle of a pattern that
# regex syntax alone cannot express.

# Expect: --->abc
#           1 ^^      b
#           2 ^ ^     c
#          0: abc
# pcre2test installs its own handler and prints one trace line per callout
# hit, showing the callout number, the position markers and the next
# pattern item. That handler is the part PHP has no way to attach.
/a(?C1)b(?C2)c/
    abc

# Expect: Callout 1  b
#         Callout "tag"  c
# Callout enumeration: pcre2_callout_enumerate() walks a compiled pattern
# and lists every callout without running a match. Useful for tooling and
# also unreachable from PHP.
/a(?C1)b(?C"tag")c/callout_info

# Expect: --->ab
#          +0 ^      a
#          +1 ^^     b
#          +2 ^ ^    End of pattern
#          0: ab
# Auto callouts: PCRE2_AUTO_CALLOUT inserts a callout before every pattern
# item, so you get a full step-by-step trace without touching the pattern.
# PHP cannot reach this one either.
/ab/auto_callout
    ab
